                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Recovery is one job. Matching is another.</h2>
                <p>The Middleborough release describes an investigation and a recovery. It does not describe how the recovered firearms were identified as the ones taken on July 25. It does not say anyone handed over serials. It does not say the recovery happened because of a list.</p>

                <p>What it does say is that recovery is possible — including after the guns have crossed a state line, and including after some of them were buried.</p>

                <p>Matching a recovered firearm back to a specific owner is a different job. That is serial-number work. Police enter stolen guns into systems such as the National Crime Information Center (NCIC). When a gun turns up later, the serial is what lets an officer connect it to a report. Without make, model, caliber, and serial, “we recovered some guns” does not automatically become “these are yours.”</p>

                <h2 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold text-gray-900">Keep a copy you can still open</h2>
                <p>If the house was just burgled, you need a copy of make, model, caliber, and serials that is not sitting in the room that was just emptied. You need a list you can still open from another device, from a printed report stored somewhere else, or from a trusted person — not only from the house you just walked back into.</p>

                <p>That is a lighter version of the same records problem in <a href="when-the-safe-gets-stolen.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">Your Safe Left the House. Do You Still Have the Serials?</a>: records that have to survive the theft. This post is not that story. Those thefts are about a safe that left or was found open. This one is about what happens after recovery starts.</p>

                <p>A list in a drawer next to the guns, or only on a phone that left with a burglar, is not a copy you can open from the driveway while you wait for police.</p>

                <div class="bg-gray-100 rounded-xl p-6 my-8">
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-4"><i class="bi bi-check2-square text-av-gold mr-2"></i>Have this ready before you need it</h4>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Make / manufacturer</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Model</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Caliber or gauge</li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> <strong>Serial number</strong></li>
                        <li><i class="bi bi-check text-av-gold mr-2"></i> Photos, if you have them — overall and serial close-up</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>

                <p>That is the same-day list. Distinguishing marks and accessories help, but they are secondary to being able to identify each firearm. Check your local reporting rules; some jurisdictions set a deadline. This is not legal advice — use local law and the <a href="stolen-firearm-checklist.html" class="text-av-gold hover:underline">stolen-firearm checklist</a> for the after-theft sequence.</p>

